$DIR/main.p:21:10: s holds a view of v (v.Slice(), line 19), which was passed to run(push, &v), which may free what it looks at or move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) at line 20, so s can no longer be used here
    s looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    v.Slice() at line 19. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. A call
    that may free memory the view looks at, or move an owner out of it, through
    a pointer to the variable or to memory it owns ends the view as well. At
    line 20 v was passed to run(push, &v), which may free what it looks at or
    move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434), so
    the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value that now
    belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use s before line 20, or make that call only after the last use of s
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

